#lang turmeric refined
;; The layer-side counterpart of errors/experiment-retired-name.
;;
;; A SEMANTIC `#lang` layer is not a second enable path -- it IS its experiment,
;; scoped to one file, so `#lang turmeric refined` was exactly `--enable=refined`
;; for this file.  That pairing is why the two shims had to age out together:
;; keeping the layer shim after retiring the experiment would leave
;; `#lang turmeric refined` accepted while `--enable=refined` was refused, which
;; is precisely the inconsistency the pairing exists to prevent.
;;
;; `refined` graduated 2026-08-01 (v0.33.0) and was retired at 0.38.0, five
;; minor lines later.  GRADUATED_LAYERS[] in lang_layers.c is empty again, so an
;; unknown layer token is the hard TUR-E0330 it was before the shim -- what this
;; pins.  The table itself stays (empty) on purpose: it has to exist BEFORE the
;; next layer graduates, or that release breaks every file naming the token.
;;
;; Replaces refine-graduated-lang-layer-noop, which pinned the TUR-W0064 no-op
;; this supersedes.  Refinement checking itself is unaffected and unconditional
;; -- `#refine{...}` needs no layer and never did after graduation.
(defn main [] : int
  (println "unreachable -- the #lang line is refused first")
  0)
